WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:04.320
Hybrid cloud is one of the most common
architectural patterns out there,

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:08.480
but it's generally actually the result
of companies that are unable to abandon

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:13.360
an existing technology stack
in favor of the public cloud. But what if

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:16.960
I told you you could get the best of
both worlds? The innovation

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:20.720
and cutting edge technologies made
possible through public cloud providers,

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:24.400
and at the same time having the
flexibility and freedom of running your

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:29.359
assets where you choose to,
whether it's on-prem or edge. Today let's

00:00:29.359 --> 00:00:33.600
talk about distributed cloud and the key
benefits that it offers.

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:37.120
Now I want to start with what a general
hybrid cloud architecture

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:41.040
looks like. So, you're going to have
customers that are running things on

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:46.559
on-prem environments,
maybe they're running things on public

00:00:46.559 --> 00:00:51.760
cloud environments
or even private clouds, or even in addition

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:56.399
we might have edge
environments. Now

00:00:56.399 --> 00:01:00.719
managing all of these environments comes
with their own set of Ops teams,

00:01:00.719 --> 00:01:04.640
challenges, data locality, latency
concerns,

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:09.360
basically all of them will require some
overhead to maintain.

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:13.360
Distributed cloud comes in right at the
center.

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:18.400
Essentially what you'll have is a public
cloud

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:21.759
sitting right at the center that's able
to manage

00:01:21.759 --> 00:01:25.520
all of those other environments.
Essentially it extends the capabilities

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:30.159
of a public cloud
to other capabilities. Now i think

00:01:30.159 --> 00:01:34.240
Gartner put it best when they said,
that in a report that they recently

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:38.799
published, they said
that distributed cloud fixes what hybrid

00:01:38.799 --> 00:01:42.960
cloud
breaks. Now today I want to cover four

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:47.840
major themes that generally correspond
with what distributed cloud aims to

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:50.320
solve,
and these actually happen to line up

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:53.520
with where hybrid cloud tends to fall
short.

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:59.200
Now the first one that I want to start
with is consistency.

00:02:01.119 --> 00:02:04.799
Now as I go through the example today
I'm going to put on a number of

00:02:04.799 --> 00:02:08.319
different hats,
whether I'm Dev, Ops, or Security, and I

00:02:08.319 --> 00:02:11.760
want to start with an Ops Engineer
because an Ops Engineer

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:15.440
will generally have to be kind of
interfacing with a distributed cloud

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:17.520
environment,
and that's where they will generally

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:23.120
start. So, as an Ops Engineer,
let's say that one of the first things

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:28.800
that I want to do
is work with a cloud environment. So

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:32.800
what Ii want to do is go to a public
cloud

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:36.560
and in that environment let's say that I
want to create

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:40.319
a Kubernetes, or even an OpenShift
cluster.

00:02:40.319 --> 00:02:47.200
So, basically a way to manage
and orchestrate cloud-based applications.

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:51.120
Now in this process of creating an
OpenShift cluster

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:55.040
I'm probably given an option at one
point to choose where do I want this

00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:58.959
cluster to live.
So here's where I'm given a few choices.

00:02:58.959 --> 00:03:01.599
So, maybe i want to do it in a public
cloud location.

00:03:01.599 --> 00:03:04.959
So, let's say for example somewhere like
Dallas

00:03:04.959 --> 00:03:10.800
or London, but
through a distributed cloud I can

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:15.040
register with the public cloud
other locations. So maybe somewhere like

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:18.400
an on-prem or edge location.
So, let's say I've got an on-prem

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:22.239
location in Bangalore,

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:31.120
or an edge location
in Morocco. So basically what we've got

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:34.319
here,
is whether it's public, on-prem, or edge

00:03:34.319 --> 00:03:38.080
locations,
we can choose one of those capabilities

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:40.720
in,
directly in my public cloud UI and

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:42.879
dashboard.
That's what a distributed cloud will

00:03:42.879 --> 00:03:47.840
generally offer.
So, through this process I'm now able to

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:52.000
deploy
OpenShift container platform clusters

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:56.959
in three different regions. Let's say,
so we've got three different OCD

00:03:56.959 --> 00:04:01.840
clusters. We'll say one of them is in
public.

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:08.000
We'll say one of them is in edge.

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:17.680
And we'll say the last one is on-prem.
Okay. So, we've got that kind of situated

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:22.560
from an Ops Engineering perspective
I'm able to create and manage clusters

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:26.160
across the board
whether they're in any of these general

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:29.919
hybrid cloud style
environments. Now as an Ops Engineer that

00:04:29.919 --> 00:04:32.000
might be good for day one, but for day
two

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:35.680
I need additional capabilities. Let's say
that I want to do something like

00:04:35.680 --> 00:04:40.160
scaling up and down. Well that's one of
the key advantages of a cloud,

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:43.680
is the elasticity. So, let's say I can
scale up and down.

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:47.040
In addition, let's say I want my clusters
to be able to network and interface with

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:50.960
one another, basically communicate
my applications communicating across the

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:54.960
board. So, maybe i want to take advantage
of a service mesh capability,

00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:59.040
that's something a distributed cloud can
offer. So,

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:04.960
taking a look at that we can start doing
some service meshing capabilities.

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:08.800
Okay. So, now that we've got that
groundwork laid out, we've covered how

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:12.000
basically for an Ops Engineer I have a
consistent

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:16.720
experience for deploying clusters in any
of these locations,

00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:23.199
you have consistency. Next up,
let's talk about DevOps.

00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:27.759
So, we've covered a bit of DevOps already,
so with the Ops perspective of deploying

00:05:27.759 --> 00:05:31.120
and managing clusters,
but for developers who want to utilize

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:34.000
capabilities generally available in
public clouds,

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:37.600
well I have a different angle of
approach here.

00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:43.520
So, putting on my hat as a Dev.
Now let's say one of the first things

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:49.199
that I want to do here
is create a service, right. So

00:05:49.199 --> 00:05:54.240
generally with OCP most DevOps
capabilities are handled it's a really

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:56.720
good platform for being able to work
with containers

00:05:56.720 --> 00:06:00.160
and kind of deploying apps, but let's say
I want to take advantage of a service

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:04.840
that cloud providers generally provide.
So, this would be something like a

00:06:04.840 --> 00:06:08.560
database.
So I want to create a DB. So, I'm in my

00:06:08.560 --> 00:06:11.759
cloud provider,
I'm in the dashboard, and I want to

00:06:11.759 --> 00:06:16.800
create a database
and I'm given a similar option. I'm asked

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:20.800
where do you want to create it? And some
given options like before,

00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:25.520
data centers or locations, not just
public cloud but also on-prem or edge,

00:06:25.520 --> 00:06:29.520
and I can choose to deploy those
high-value capabilities,

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:34.000
you know so I can decide to deploy a
database in my public cloud.

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:37.520
Well this is nothing special I've been
able to do that. Or I should be able to

00:06:37.520 --> 00:06:42.560
deploy a database,
in addition into edge environments.

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:47.440
So that's taken care of as well.
So now that we've gotten that piece kind

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:50.080
of squared away,
we've kind of talked about how

00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:54.960
distributed cloud enables
DevOps to be more efficient. One other

00:06:54.960 --> 00:06:57.919
thing I want to use this example for
here

00:06:57.919 --> 00:07:02.479
is innovation, and this is for high
value services.

00:07:02.479 --> 00:07:06.240
So, let's say I want to do AI or machine
learning capabilities,

00:07:06.240 --> 00:07:10.160
something that a public cloud will
generally offer. So, you know, let's say I

00:07:10.160 --> 00:07:15.759
have some AI or ML
capabilities that I want to deploy. Well

00:07:15.759 --> 00:07:19.039
same as before,
I can take those capabilities and deploy

00:07:19.039 --> 00:07:24.160
them to any of these
environments. So with distributed cloud

00:07:24.160 --> 00:07:27.199
you're able to innovate
with capabilities generally only

00:07:27.199 --> 00:07:30.639
available in public cloud,
in environments outside of the public

00:07:30.639 --> 00:07:37.360
cloud. It's one of the key capabilities.
The last one I want to talk about today

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:40.080
is governance.

00:07:40.319 --> 00:07:44.800
Now governance is a key requirement for
pretty much all companies, but especially

00:07:44.800 --> 00:07:48.400
you know financial institutions, or
health or insurance companies,

00:07:48.400 --> 00:07:54.479
basically concerns like data regulations,
compliance, data locality.

00:07:54.479 --> 00:07:59.759
How can a Security Admin effectively and
with a single control plane

00:07:59.759 --> 00:08:06.080
manage policies across the board? Well
time to put on my last hat here. Let's

00:08:06.080 --> 00:08:11.759
say that I'm a Security Admin
and I've created a network policy.

00:08:11.759 --> 00:08:17.120
So, this network policy, let's say that it
handles things like RBAC,

00:08:17.120 --> 00:08:20.960
or network policies across the board, or
security policies,

00:08:20.960 --> 00:08:24.879
and I want to deploy them to clusters
everywhere in my business,

00:08:24.879 --> 00:08:28.160
not just in the cloud, but also on-prem
and edge.

00:08:28.160 --> 00:08:32.240
Well, with the distributed cloud you can
do exactly that.

00:08:32.240 --> 00:08:36.000
By taking advantage of the capabilities
made available I can deploy these

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:38.959
policies
across the board to any of these

00:08:38.959 --> 00:08:41.680
environments. Now this is a simple
example,

00:08:41.680 --> 00:08:46.000
but it goes to show you how distributed
cloud is tackling problems

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:50.800
that hybrid cloud generally runs into.
Thanks for joining me for this quick

00:08:50.800 --> 00:08:53.920
overview of distributed cloud.
